WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.410
<v Jonathon>For two years I took 1604 home at
six-fifteen, on purpose, to miss bath time.

2
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:09.890
<v Wes Hart>1604 at six-fifteen is a parking lot.

3
00:00:10.082 --> 00:00:11.470
<v Jonathon>I picked the parking lot.

4
00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:15.732
<v Jonathon>Then one night my wife goes,
"Since when do you like 1604?"

5
00:00:18.382 --> 00:00:21.370
<v Wes Hart>This is Walk Steadfast. I'm Wes Hart.

6
00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.687
<v Wes Hart>I've sat at tables with
men for twenty-some years.

7
00:00:25.007 --> 00:00:25.930
<v Jonathon>I'm Jonathon.

8
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:29.247
<v Jonathon>I came to faith at twenty-nine,
the year I met Wes.

9
00:00:29.437 --> 00:00:35.090
<v Wes Hart>Starting today, twice a week, we talk
straight about following Jesus as a man.

10
00:00:35.431 --> 00:00:39.450
<v Jonathon>Wes wrote a book called Steadfast Men,
and a video training goes with it.

11
00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:41.931
<v Jonathon>Both are at walksteadfast.com.

12
00:00:42.285 --> 00:00:46.361
<v Wes Hart>Today we ask why good intentions
don't make it to Tuesday.

13
00:00:46.853 --> 00:00:47.903
<v Jonathon>Episode one.

14
00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:51.126
<v Wes Hart>Tuesday, January fifth,
twenty twenty-seven.

15
00:00:51.447 --> 00:00:53.328
<v Jonathon>The Man You Keep Saying You'll Be.

16
00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:55.822
<v Wes Hart>Pull up a chair.

17
00:00:56.113 --> 00:00:57.450
<v Jonathon>Sorry. Sorry.

18
00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:01.450
<v Jonathon>My daughter just texted me "good
luck daddy" and a picture of a dog.

19
00:01:01.923 --> 00:01:03.022
<v Wes Hart>You don't have a dog.

20
00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.560
<v Jonathon>We don't have a dog. I
think it's a negotiation.

21
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.400
<v Wes Hart>Well. Welcome.

22
00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:12.200
<v Wes Hart>This is the first one, so I want to tell
you what's going to happen today, because

23
00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.177
<v Wes Hart>it's a little longer than
what we'll normally do.

24
00:01:15.503 --> 00:01:16.143
<v Jonathon>A little.

25
00:01:16.634 --> 00:01:20.770
<v Wes Hart>A little. Three things. First, who we are.

26
00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.760
<v Wes Hart>You should know who you're sitting with.

27
00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:29.050
<v Wes Hart>Then we're going to actually do the thing.
A real conversation. About Tuesdays.

28
00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:34.760
<v Wes Hart>And before we let you go, what this whole
thing is, the book, the training, the

29
00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:39.450
<v Wes Hart>website, all of it, laid out once
so you never have to wonder.

30
00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:42.266
<v Jonathon>About one Tuesday in
particular, which is today.

31
00:01:42.755 --> 00:01:43.610
<v Wes Hart>Which is today.

32
00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:47.356
<v Wes Hart>And the question underneath
all of it is pretty simple.

33
00:01:47.705 --> 00:01:52.370
<v Wes Hart>What kind of man are you on a
regular day? Not your best day.

34
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:56.195
<v Wes Hart>Not the day with the pictures. A Tuesday.

35
00:01:56.623 --> 00:02:00.710
<v Jonathon>And I'll tell you right now, my answer to
that question was bad for a long time.

36
00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:01.854
<v Wes Hart>Mine too.

37
00:02:02.327 --> 00:02:06.230
<v Wes Hart>And if you're twenty-two and nobody's
waiting on you at home, stay.

38
00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:11.364
<v Wes Hart>Your Tuesday's in here too.
We'll get to you before the half.

39
00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:12.510
<v Jonathon>We'll get there.

40
00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:16.560
<v Jonathon>But first, people need to know who you
are, and you're terrible at talking about

41
00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:17.295
<v Jonathon>yourself.

42
00:02:17.574 --> 00:02:18.499
<v Wes Hart>I am.

43
00:02:18.834 --> 00:02:22.150
<v Jonathon>So I'm going to do it, and you
can fix whatever I get wrong.

44
00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:23.170
<v Wes Hart>Go ahead.

45
00:02:24.413 --> 00:02:28.570
<v Jonathon>Okay, so. This is Wes Hart.
He's fifty-two years old.

46
00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:33.960
<v Jonathon>Wes lives out in the Hill Country, west of
San Antonio, on a few acres, in a town he

47
00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:35.420
<v Jonathon>won't let me say on the air.

48
00:02:35.777 --> 00:02:36.277
<v Wes Hart>Correct.

49
00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.870
<v Jonathon>He's been married twenty-seven
years. He's got three kids.

50
00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:43.440
<v Jonathon>A daughter who's twenty-five,
married, teaches second grade.

51
00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:47.560
<v Jonathon>A son who's twenty-one, finishing
up an electrical apprenticeship.

52
00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:52.210
<v Jonathon>And a sixteen-year-old who just got his
license, which is why Wes looks like that.

53
00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:56.550
<v Wes Hart>He drives like he's late for something.
He's never late for anything.

54
00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:00.110
<v Wes Hart>He's got nowhere to be.
He just drives like it.

55
00:03:00.361 --> 00:03:01.331
<v Jonathon>You rode with him?

56
00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.030
<v Wes Hart>I rode with him Saturday.

57
00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:06.790
<v Wes Hart>I held the handle above the door
the whole way to the feed store.

58
00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:10.396
<v Wes Hart>The handle's there for a
reason. I found the reason.

59
00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:14.736
<v Jonathon>And what does Wes do for a living?
I've known this man seven years.

60
00:03:14.856 --> 00:03:15.790
<v Wes Hart>I work.

61
00:03:16.039 --> 00:03:17.010
<v Jonathon>He works.

62
00:03:17.138 --> 00:03:18.040
<v Wes Hart>Same as you.

63
00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:23.200
<v Wes Hart>I get up early, I come home tired,
my truck has too many miles on it.

64
00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:25.117
<v Wes Hart>That's all anybody needs.

65
00:03:25.351 --> 00:03:26.760
<v Jonathon>That's going to be a running thing.

66
00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.742
<v Jonathon>People are going to ask in the comments.

67
00:03:29.047 --> 00:03:30.290
<v Wes Hart>Let them ask.

68
00:03:30.472 --> 00:03:33.300
<v Jonathon>Okay. Here's the part
that matters, though.

69
00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:37.560
<v Jonathon>For twenty-something years, Wes has
been sitting across tables from men.

70
00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:41.490
<v Jonathon>Kitchen tables, church
fellowship halls, deer camp.

71
00:03:41.623 --> 00:03:44.910
<v Wes Hart>Deer camp's the best
one. Nobody can leave.

72
00:03:45.026 --> 00:03:49.060
<v Jonathon>And a couple years ago he wrote down what
he'd learned at all those tables, and

73
00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:53.730
<v Jonathon>that's the book. We'll get to the
book. That's my version. Now fix it.

74
00:03:53.778 --> 00:03:54.920
<v Wes Hart>That's mostly right.

75
00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.790
<v Wes Hart>You left out the part where I wasn't
any good at this for a long time.

76
00:03:58.947 --> 00:04:00.170
<v Jonathon>I was getting there.

77
00:04:00.311 --> 00:04:05.660
<v Wes Hart>You were being nice. I grew up on my
granddad's place. My dad was a steady man.

78
00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:06.300
<v Wes Hart>Quiet.

79
00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:10.270
<v Wes Hart>He was at every game I ever played,
and he almost never said what he felt.

80
00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:13.650
<v Wes Hart>I loved him. He loved me. I knew that.

81
00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:18.020
<v Wes Hart>But the first time I heard him say "I'm
proud of you," he was in a hospital bed, and

82
00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.210
<v Wes Hart>I was forty-three.

83
00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:22.280
<v Wes Hart>He died nine years ago.

84
00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:26.430
<v Wes Hart>And I want to be careful here, because
that's not a sad story about a bad dad.

85
00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:29.600
<v Wes Hart>He was a good dad. It's
a different kind of ache.

86
00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:33.630
<v Wes Hart>A good man who never said it out loud.
I've met a lot of men with that one.

87
00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:34.450
<v Jonathon>Yeah.

88
00:04:34.563 --> 00:04:35.970
<v Wes Hart>And then my thirties.

89
00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:40.620
<v Wes Hart>I had a decent job, I coached Little League,
I sat in the third row at church every

90
00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:45.690
<v Wes Hart>Sunday. And I was gone. In my own living
room. My wife has a name for those years.

91
00:04:46.026 --> 00:04:47.370
<v Jonathon>The fog years.

92
00:04:47.818 --> 00:04:52.390
<v Wes Hart>The fog years. She saw it before I
did. I want that on the record early.

93
00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:56.123
<v Wes Hart>She saw it first, and she was
patient a lot longer than I deserved.

94
00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.170
<v Wes Hart>And I know that's not how
it goes for every man.

95
00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.760
<v Wes Hart>Some of you, she stopped
asking, and then she left.

96
00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:06.610
<v Wes Hart>I'm not going to pretend my ending is the
only one. You've got a chair here too.

97
00:05:07.019 --> 00:05:08.770
<v Jonathon>And then the man with the mug.

98
00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:11.690
<v Wes Hart>And then the man with the mug.

99
00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:16.960
<v Wes Hart>When I was thirty-six, I went to a men's
breakfast I didn't want to go to, and there

100
00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:22.030
<v Wes Hart>was an older man there. I call
him Mr. Abernathy. Everybody did.

101
00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:25.550
<v Wes Hart>He'd been a lineman for the
power company forty years.

102
00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:30.060
<v Wes Hart>And he looked across that table at me, didn't
know me from anybody, and asked me one

103
00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.019
<v Wes Hart>question.

104
00:05:31.282 --> 00:05:32.769
<v Wes Hart>"What are you waiting on?"

105
00:05:33.828 --> 00:05:38.536
<v Wes Hart>I didn't have an answer. I had a lot
of words. I didn't have an answer.

106
00:05:38.897 --> 00:05:40.410
<v Jonathon>And he didn't let it go.

107
00:05:40.829 --> 00:05:44.530
<v Wes Hart>He met me every other Thursday
for six years. Same diner.

108
00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:49.160
<v Wes Hart>He drank his coffee out of this exact mug,
and when he died at eighty-eight, the

109
00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:53.060
<v Wes Hart>woman who ran the place wrapped it in a
paper towel and handed it to me at the

110
00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:53.799
<v Wes Hart>funeral.

111
00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:55.330
<v Jonathon>I didn't know that part.

112
00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:58.780
<v Wes Hart>I don't tell it much. So that's who I am.

113
00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:03.380
<v Wes Hart>I'm a man who wasted some years and got
pulled out of it by an old lineman who kept

114
00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:04.670
<v Wes Hart>asking a question.

115
00:06:04.981 --> 00:06:09.037
<v Jonathon>So why are you doing this? Why a show?
You could just keep doing the tables.

116
00:06:09.436 --> 00:06:12.030
<v Wes Hart>Because most men never get to a table.

117
00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:16.720
<v Wes Hart>I've had the same conversation with hundreds
of men, and for every one who sat down,

118
00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:19.510
<v Wes Hart>there's ten who would have,
if they'd known it existed.

119
00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:23.550
<v Wes Hart>And because I'm fifty-two now, and
the man who sat with me is gone.

120
00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.583
<v Wes Hart>Somebody has to pull up the chair.

121
00:06:25.997 --> 00:06:27.412
<v Jonathon>And what do you bring?

122
00:06:27.698 --> 00:06:29.630
<v Wes Hart>The long view, I guess.

123
00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:33.980
<v Wes Hart>I've been married twenty-seven years, so
I've been wrong in most of the ways you can

124
00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:35.290
<v Wes Hart>be wrong in a marriage.

125
00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:39.610
<v Wes Hart>I've raised three kids, and I've
been wrong in all of those ways too.

126
00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:44.450
<v Wes Hart>If you're in a season, odds are I've been
through it or sat with a man who was.

127
00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:47.930
<v Wes Hart>And I'll tell you when I got
it wrong. That's the deal.

128
00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:51.072
<v Jonathon>He will. He tells on himself constantly.

129
00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:53.470
<v Wes Hart>It's the only thing I'm consistent at.

130
00:06:54.789 --> 00:06:56.347
<v Wes Hart>Now. My turn.

131
00:06:56.556 --> 00:06:57.490
<v Jonathon>Be nice.

132
00:06:57.608 --> 00:06:59.030
<v Wes Hart>I'm always nice.

133
00:06:59.208 --> 00:07:01.410
<v Jonathon>You are not always nice.

134
00:07:01.631 --> 00:07:02.700
<v Wes Hart>This is Jonathon.

135
00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:09.293
<v Wes Hart>He's thirty-six, thirty-seven in
February. He grew up in Pasadena, Texas.

136
00:07:09.576 --> 00:07:10.670
<v Jonathon>Here we go.

137
00:07:10.742 --> 00:07:14.880
<v Wes Hart>Pasadena, Texas, for those of you
not from here, is east of Houston.

138
00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:18.970
<v Wes Hart>Refinery country. Flat.
It smells like work.

139
00:07:19.248 --> 00:07:24.170
<v Jonathon>It's a great place. Nothing's the Hill
Country, I know. Everyone's been told.

140
00:07:24.533 --> 00:07:28.555
<v Wes Hart>He lives on the north side of San
Antonio now, in a subdivision.

141
00:07:29.007 --> 00:07:33.120
<v Wes Hart>Jonathon is a regional sales manager
for a company that sells commercial air

142
00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:34.530
<v Wes Hart>conditioning equipment.

143
00:07:34.942 --> 00:07:37.430
<v Jonathon>Which in Texas is the
easiest pitch in the world.

144
00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:39.581
<v Jonathon>"Would you like to not die in August?"

145
00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:42.110
<v Wes Hart>He's been married ten years to Dani.

146
00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:46.594
<v Wes Hart>She's a labor and delivery nurse,
and she works nights, three a week.

147
00:07:46.873 --> 00:07:50.170
<v Jonathon>She's the best person I know.
You can put that on a card.

148
00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:52.090
<v Wes Hart>And they've got two kids.

149
00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:57.544
<v Wes Hart>Ruby, who's seven and runs the house, and
Theo, who's four and runs into things.

150
00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:00.055
<v Jonathon>That's accurate. That is accurate.

151
00:08:00.507 --> 00:08:04.090
<v Wes Hart>He pitched in college, small
school, until his shoulder went.

152
00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:06.870
<v Wes Hart>Now he coaches Ruby's coach-pitch team.

153
00:08:07.106 --> 00:08:08.690
<v Jonathon>Badly. Say it.

154
00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:10.187
<v Wes Hart>With a lot of energy.

155
00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:14.070
<v Jonathon>Last fall we went oh-and-six.
We've never won a game.

156
00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:17.960
<v Jonathon>Ruby picked this watch for me at the end
of the season, and it does not tell time

157
00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:18.676
<v Jonathon>correctly.

158
00:08:19.064 --> 00:08:20.029
<v Wes Hart>Does it beep?

159
00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:23.833
<v Jonathon>Every hour. On the hour.
It's beeped in church.

160
00:08:24.233 --> 00:08:26.614
<v Wes Hart>That's my version. Fix it.

161
00:08:26.624 --> 00:08:31.560
<v Jonathon>Okay. That's the fun part. Here's the
rest of it. My dad left when I was nine.

162
00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:33.600
<v Jonathon>It was a Saturday, in October.

163
00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:38.120
<v Jonathon>I remember his truck had a dent in the
tailgate, and I remember watching the dent go

164
00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:42.550
<v Jonathon>down the street. That's the thing
I remember. Not his face. The dent.

165
00:08:43.708 --> 00:08:47.760
<v Jonathon>He's alive. He lives in Lake Charles.
He calls about twice a year.

166
00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:51.320
<v Jonathon>And I'm going to tell you right up
front, I have not figured that out.

167
00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:55.200
<v Jonathon>We're going to talk about dads on this
show, and I'm going to be the guy who hasn't

168
00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:56.170
<v Jonathon>healed on schedule.

169
00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:57.783
<v Wes Hart>That's allowed here.

170
00:08:57.976 --> 00:09:02.970
<v Jonathon>My mom worked two jobs, and by eleven
she was calling me the man of the house.

171
00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:04.630
<v Jonathon>She meant it as a compliment.

172
00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:08.220
<v Jonathon>She's a great woman, she did
everything right that she could do.

173
00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:10.986
<v Jonathon>But eleven's young to
be the man of anything.

174
00:09:11.319 --> 00:09:12.060
<v Wes Hart>It is.

175
00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:16.590
<v Jonathon>And then my twenties. I was good
at sales. Like, really good.

176
00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:19.580
<v Jonathon>I was good at the room. I
lived for the leaderboard.

177
00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:24.490
<v Jonathon>And I drank too much on weeknights to
come down from the day. Nothing dramatic.

178
00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:26.450
<v Jonathon>No arrests, no wrecks.

179
00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:30.580
<v Jonathon>Just every night, a little too
much, and I called it unwinding.

180
00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:32.234
<v Wes Hart>When did that turn?

181
00:09:32.569 --> 00:09:34.760
<v Jonathon>At twenty-nine. Dani
was pregnant with Ruby.

182
00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:38.760
<v Jonathon>And she sat down across from me at our
kitchen table one night, and she was so calm

183
00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:42.890
<v Jonathon>about it, which was the scary part.
She said, "I'm not raising two kids."

184
00:09:43.024 --> 00:09:43.729
<v Wes Hart>Yeah.

185
00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:45.470
<v Jonathon>And she wasn't wrong.

186
00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:50.340
<v Jonathon>About a month later, a customer of mine,
a mechanical contractor, invited me to a

187
00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:51.830
<v Jonathon>men's breakfast at his church.

188
00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:55.190
<v Jonathon>And I went because I couldn't come
up with an excuse fast enough.

189
00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:58.172
<v Wes Hart>That's how most men end
up at a men's breakfast.

190
00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.870
<v Jonathon>I sat in the parking
lot for eleven minutes.

191
00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:04.790
<v Jonathon>I know because I watched the clock
on the dash. And I almost left.

192
00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:08.660
<v Jonathon>And when I finally went in, there was one
open seat, and it was across from this

193
00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:09.170
<v Jonathon>guy.

194
00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:12.704
<v Wes Hart>I remember you. You had
a company quarter-zip on.

195
00:10:13.031 --> 00:10:14.290
<v Jonathon>I still have it.

196
00:10:14.473 --> 00:10:17.011
<v Wes Hart>I know. You wear it on this show.

197
00:10:17.147 --> 00:10:18.790
<v Jonathon>I'm going to wear it on this show.

198
00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:21.030
<v Jonathon>And I didn't say anything
to him that morning.

199
00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:24.810
<v Jonathon>Three months later I sent him a text
asking if he'd get breakfast with me.

200
00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.477
<v Jonathon>That is the scariest text
I've ever sent a man.

201
00:10:27.949 --> 00:10:32.482
<v Wes Hart>It said, "Hey, this is Jonathon from the
breakfast, the HVAC guy, no pressure."

202
00:10:32.924 --> 00:10:34.810
<v Jonathon>"No pressure" means a lot of pressure.

203
00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:36.550
<v Wes Hart>It means a lot of pressure.

204
00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:40.590
<v Jonathon>And that was seven years ago.
We've met most Thursdays since.

205
00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:42.020
<v Jonathon>I came to faith that year.

206
00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.560
<v Jonathon>And I haven't had a
weeknight drink in six years.

207
00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:49.210
<v Jonathon>Nothing dramatic about that either.
